# `golem/net`

**Networking:** `Listener` owns the primary integrated transport selected by `Config.Transport`: WebSocket (`/ws` by default) or WebTransport (`/wt` by default). `WebSocketHandler` is always available so a WebTransport-primary listener can mount a fallback on a caller-owned HTTP server; both handlers share session IDs, hooks, snapshots, sends, statistics, and shutdown. It manages per-client `Session`s, optional static/map file serving, and WebTransport datagrams. Imports **`golem/registry`** for entity snapshots; does **not** import `golem/` (loop/Server). The parent **`golem`** package normally creates and owns the `Listener`, but callers can mount it on custom servers.

**Transport boundary:** `reliableMessageChannel` is the transport-neutral reliable stream lane used by `Session.writeBatch`. `datagramChannel` is the raw WebTransport datagram substrate. `Session` owns the datagram reliability protocol on top of that substrate, including ACK packets, resend state, and ordered delivery for reliable datagram lanes. The datagram protocol header now includes a fixed 128-bit selective-ACK field, so payload budgets in `payload.go` and `golem-js/src/client.ts` must stay aligned with the shared header size.

**Reliable batching:** `SendBatch` / `BroadcastBatch` queue one reliable batch per session, which keeps channel pressure low even when many entities update in one tick. Reliable logical frames are bounded to 256 KiB. WebSocket coalesces raw logical frames into ordered binary messages up to that cap. WebTransport coalesces length-prefixed logical frames into ordered writes targeting 32000-byte chunks; a single larger logical frame remains one write. `maxReliableMessageBytes` applies per logical frame, not per transport write.

**Datagram lanes:** WebTransport now has three logical datagram lanes:
- raw `unreliable` datagrams exposed through `OnDatagram` and `SendUnreliable`
- `reliable-unordered` datagrams exposed through dedicated listener/server hooks and send APIs
- `reliable-ordered` datagrams exposed through dedicated listener/server hooks and send APIs

Reliable datagrams are for small discrete messages such as explicit command/custom payloads. Automatic incremental entity replication uses the parent `golem.Server` `StateUpdateLaneDatagram` path by default, which sends compact state records on eventual-state datagrams. Snapshots, world data, server events, spawn/removal traffic, and oversized or full-state payloads remain on the reliable stream path. `StateUpdateLaneStream` keeps incremental entity replication on the reliable stream instead.

In a mixed listener, automatic datagram replication is selected per session: WebTransport receives eventual-state datagrams while WebSocket receives equivalent wrapped deltas on its reliable stream. Targeted datagram sends to WebSocket return the existing unsupported error. Datagram broadcasts retain the pure-WebSocket unsupported error, but a WebTransport-primary mixed listener skips WebSocket sessions and delivers to capable sessions.

**Scheduling and slow clients:** `Session` no longer multiplexes all outbound work through one FIFO. It keeps separate stream/unreliable/reliable-datagram queues, prioritizes ACK control work first, then fresh unreliable datagrams, then reliable ordered/unordered datagrams, and finally stream writes. Reliable datagrams now drain in guarded bulk passes instead of the old small fixed burst: each pass prefers fresh reliable datagrams ahead of retries, stops only when work runs out or the per-pass send/time guardrails hit, and otherwise resumes remaining work on the next wake or `datagramSchedulerInterval` tick. Queue overflow still logs and forcibly closes the slow session, but the clean transport handshake runs asynchronously so a tick-time send never waits on a slow peer. Once a session is closing, later sends and inbound hook delivery are dropped. `CloseWithReason` waits for the clean transport handshake; latency-sensitive tick/HTTP callers use `RequestCloseWithReason`, which atomically marks closing and signals shutdown before returning, then performs the reasoned WebSocket/WebTransport handshake asynchronously. `Session.IsClosing` exposes that monotonic, concurrency-safe state so delayed work can stop before attaching gameplay state while `OnDisconnect` is still pending. Close is idempotent and first-request-wins, including the reason.

**Backlog inspection:** `Session.OutboundSnapshot` and `Listener.OutboundBacklogForLog` report stream and unreliable channel depths, reliable datagram pending vs in-flight counts, the age of the head of each reliable queue, and per-interval `d_1s` (successful `WriteDatagram` calls) / `st_1s` (successful reliable stream `WriteBatch` invocations) since the last `OutboundBacklogForLog` call. With `LogReplicationStats`, `golem.Server` prints an additional `golem: net outbound backlog` line each second after the replication line; the first line after connect has zero deltas.

**Snapshots and wrappers:** `messageWrapper` wraps reliable stream entity frames (`WrapEntityUpdate` from `golem`), while compact state datagram records bypass `ServerMessage` / `EntityUpdate` wrappers. World snapshot frames are already fully wrapped and bypass the wrapper. `sendSnapshot` sends world data before entity snapshots and skips entity snapshots entirely when `interestEnabled` is true. Optional `SetEntitySnapshotFunc` provides session-aware `[]EntitySnapshot` (entity ID + unwrapped FullUpdate, which may already be owner-redacted by the parent `golem.Server` provider); nil retains `registry.SnapshotAll()` for low-level Listener users. Optional `SetEntitySnapshotCompleteFunc` receives delivered entity IDs only after every entity snapshot frame succeeds — partial failures close the connection without committing known state.

**Hooks and data:** `OnUpgrade(func(*http.Request) (any, error))` runs synchronously before transport acceptance and seeds `Session.Data`; a non-nil error rejects with HTTP 401 (`err.Error()` is logged and written to the response body — never put tokens/secrets in upgrade errors). For the common token-in-query-param handshake, prefer `golem/auth.UpgradeHandler` (policy-free; does not import `golem/net`). `Session.RemoteAddr` and informational `Session.Transport` are populated from the upgrade request before `OnConnect` fires. Runtime routing and chunk statistics use the session's immutable internal physical-transport identity, so user mutation of `Session.Transport` cannot change capabilities. `OnConnect`, `OnMessage`, `OnDatagram`, `OnReliableUnordered`, `OnReliableOrdered`, and `OnDisconnect` are listener hooks that the parent `golem.Server` usually replaces with enqueue proxies so gameplay callbacks run on the tick goroutine instead of connection goroutines. `OnDatagram` must keep its existing meaning: raw unreliable application payloads only; protocol ACK/control packets and reliable datagrams are decoded before hooks fire.

**Endpoint and shutdown pieces:** `Handler()` remains the configured-primary endpoint for compatibility. For WebTransport, pair it with `WebTransportServer` on a caller-owned HTTP/3 server; mount `WebSocketHandler()` separately for fallback. The WebSocket handler clears inherited caller-owned HTTP `ReadTimeout`/`WriteTimeout` deadlines before upgrade so they cannot terminate a long-lived session. `CloseSessions` permanently fences new admission, tracks each handler from authorization through physical acceptance and snapshot delivery under the same closing gate, includes pending and connected sessions, closes both transports, and waits for handler cleanup without a late `WaitGroup.Add` race; it is called when the integrated listener stops and when `Server.Run` exits, including an already-cancelled run. Certificate hash accessors deep-copy both slices and byte values. `SetWebTransportCheckOrigin`, TLS helpers, certificate hashes, and datagram support all live here.
